  "rhythmInsightBandSteady": "A steady rhythm",
  "rhythmInsightBandDense": "A dense rhythm",
  "rhythmInsightBandVeryDense": "Almost no days off",
  "rhythmInsightHowTitle": "Where this number comes from",
  "rhythmInsightWindow": "Average over the last four weeks. Days with strength work count; cardio-only days do not. This is not a goal and not a grade — just your current rhythm.",
  "rhythmInsightWhatTitle": "Active recovery week",
  "rhythmInsightWhatBody": "Not a pause — a week at half power: working weights and set counts down 30–50%. You can skip strength entirely and keep cardio or any other activity. Muscle recovers faster than joints, ligaments, and tendons — this week gives them time to catch up.",
  "rhythmInsightPaceTitle": "What this means",
  "rhythmInsightAdviceLight": "There are enough rest days between sessions that fatigue does not stack. No need to plan active recovery yet — at this rhythm the body recovers on its own.",
  "rhythmInsightAdviceSteady": "A steady rhythm you can live on for years. If the sessions are hard, take an active recovery week every few months — better early than after the weights stall.",
  "rhythmInsightAdviceDense": "Sessions sit close together, and fatigue builds quietly. An active recovery week every two or three months usually keeps progress better than grinding without a break.",
  "rhythmInsightAdviceVeryDense": "Almost every day under load. That can work if sleep and food keep up. At this rhythm an active recovery week about once a month is ordinary care for joints and ligaments, not a cop-out.",
  "rhythmInsightPraiseSteady": "This is the rhythm people keep for years.",
